# Tauri's build script (tauri-build -> tauri_utils::acl::build::
# autogenerate_command_permissions) regenerates these permission manifests on
# every `cargo check`/`cargo build` via `write_if_changed`, which does a raw
# byte comparison against freshly generated content and rewrites the file on
# any mismatch. The generated content always uses LF.
#
# Without this override, `core.autocrlf=true` (this project's Windows
# baseline, see CLAUDE.md) checks these files out with CRLF. The very first
# build then finds the on-disk CRLF bytes don't match the LF it generates,
# rewrites the file back to LF (byte-identical to the committed blob), and
# `git status` reports it modified anyway — because those LF bytes no longer
# match what a CRLF checkout would have produced. Every worktree ends up
# permanently dirty across all 130+ autogenerated files (#472).
#
# Pinning `eol=lf` makes checkout itself produce LF, matching what the build
# script writes, so there is nothing left for a build to "fix" and nothing
# left for git to flag.
src-tauri/permissions/autogenerated/*.toml text eol=lf
